%% Which RabbitMQ plugins the broker starts with.
%%
%% Honest framing first, because the alternative is a comment that flatters
%% the change: the pinned image ALREADY ships exactly this list. Verified --
%% `cat /etc/rabbitmq/enabled_plugins` inside
%% rabbitmq@sha256:ce634665a8e7262384a793581c20cb8a46343887446eb391b77c814febeca4b2
%% prints `[rabbitmq_management,rabbitmq_prometheus].`, and a broker started
%% from that image with no mount at all already serves :15692/metrics. So this
%% file does not switch the exporter on. It states, in the repository, which
%% two plugins the monitoring stack depends on, so that a later image bump
%% that drops one fails loudly here instead of leaving Prometheus scraping a
%% closed port and every queue-depth alert silently never firing.
%%
%%   rabbitmq_management  - the HTTP management API on 15672. Deliberately not
%%                          published; it is how an operator inspects queues
%%                          from inside the stack (`docker compose exec`), and
%%                          it is the reason the image carries the
%%                          `-management` tag at all.
%%   rabbitmq_prometheus  - the exporter on 15692. This is the ONLY honest
%%                          source of queue and dead-letter depth. No
%%                          application metric can report it: a consumer that
%%                          has crashed cannot tell you how far behind it is,
%%                          and that is precisely the moment the number
%%                          matters.
%%
%% Both plugins live inside the pinned image -- verified:
%% /plugins/rabbitmq_management-4.3.6, /plugins/rabbitmq_management_agent-4.3.6
%% and /plugins/rabbitmq_prometheus-4.3.6 are present. Enabling them downloads
%% nothing, which is what makes this safe on an air-gapped host.
%%
%% Why BOTH are named even though only the exporter is new: this file is
%% bind-mounted over /etc/rabbitmq/enabled_plugins, so it REPLACES the image's
%% copy rather than adding to it. Listing only rabbitmq_prometheus here would
%% turn the management API off, and that failure surfaces much later as a 404
%% from a diagnostic command rather than as a start-up error.
%%
%% Format: an Erlang term read by file:consult/1. One list, and the trailing
%% period is required -- without it RabbitMQ cannot parse the file and starts
%% with no plugins at all. `%%` lines are comments and are ignored.
[rabbitmq_management,rabbitmq_prometheus].
